          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Protein Powder Kosher? What the Symbols Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            A protein powder is kosher when a certifying agency has checked its ingredients, its suppliers and the equipment it is made on, and allows the company to print the agency's symbol. There is no government kosher standard in the US; the symbols are trademarks owned by private agencies, and each agency sets its own policies. The symbol is the claim. Words such as kosher in an Amazon title, or kosher-style in a description, are not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The common agency symbols</h3>

          <p>
            The largest US agencies include the Orthodox Union (the U inside a circle, often called OU), OK Kosher (a K inside a circle), Star-K (a K inside a star), Kof-K (a K inside the Hebrew letter kaf) and cRc, the Chicago Rabbinical Council. Triangle K and many regional and international agencies also certify supplements. Which agencies you accept is a question of your community's practice and your rabbi's guidance, not something a buyer's guide can settle; our job is to show you what each product actually carries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dairy, DE and pareve</h3>

          <p>
            The letters next to a symbol matter more than the symbol for protein powder. A D, as in OU-D, means the product contains dairy ingredients or was made on dairy equipment. Some agencies use DE to flag dairy equipment only, with no dairy ingredient; many people who keep kosher treat DE products as permissible after meat with some restrictions, but practice varies. Pareve, sometimes spelled parve, means the agency considers the product neither meat nor dairy, so it can be eaten with either. An unmarked agency symbol on a plant product often means pareve, but agencies differ in how they mark it, so check rather than assume.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The plain K</h3>

          <p>
            A plain capital K on a label is not a trademark, because a single letter cannot be registered. It can mean a reliable agency certifies the product without printing its logo, or it can mean the manufacturer considers the product kosher with no outside supervision at all. If you see only a K, contact the company and ask which agency certifies it.
          </p>

