                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pea, rice, egg white powders</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plain K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No trademark; ask who certifies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="is-whey-kosher" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Whey Protein Kosher? Cheese, Rennet and Dairy Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey can be kosher, but it is one of the harder ingredients for an agency to certify, and it is always dairy. Whey is the liquid left over from cheesemaking. Cheese is often made with rennet, and kosher law has specific rules about cheese production, including supervision of the cheesemaking itself. That means whey is only as kosher as the cheese plant it came from, and an agency certifying a protein powder has to trace the whey back to a supervised or approved source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Agencies do not all apply the same standard to whey, and that is one reason the same raw whey may be acceptable to one agency and not another. The OU and other large agencies certify whey suppliers at the dairy level so that powder brands can buy pre-approved whey, and the OU has written publicly about whey supply shortages as demand for protein products has grown. For a buyer, the consequence is simple: you cannot infer kosher status from the ingredients of a whey powder. Two isolates with identical labels can differ completely on certification.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chalav Yisrael</h3>

          <p>
            Some observant consumers require chalav Yisrael (cholov Yisroel) dairy, meaning milk supervised from milking. Most mainstream US certifications of whey rely on the position that government-regulated milk is acceptable, often called chalav stam, and do not meet a chalav Yisrael standard unless the label says so. We did not find a chalav Yisrael whey protein powder among mainstream Amazon brands; if you keep that standard, look for the explicit marking and ask your agency or rabbi.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein, milk protein isolate and blends follow the same logic: all dairy, all needing a traceable source. Our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> and <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> rankings cover those categories on nutrition, but not every product in them is kosher certified.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pareve-options" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Pareve Protein Powder: What to Drink After a Meat Meal
          </h2>

          <p>
            The practical kosher problem with protein powder is timing. Many people who keep kosher wait a set time after eating meat before eating dairy; customs range from one hour to six, with six hours common. A whey shake is dairy, so a post-workout shake after a meat lunch can be off-limits for most of the afternoon. A pareve protein powder solves that, and it is the main reason kosher-keeping lifters own two tubs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plant proteins</h3>

          <p>
            Pea, rice, hemp and soy proteins are plant foods and are pareve in principle, but the finished powder needs certification, because flavors, creamers and the production line can introduce dairy. Some plant powders are made on equipment that also runs whey, which can earn a DE marking. Orgain and NOW Sports Pea are both listed as kosher, but neither brand states pareve in what we found, so check the symbol on the tub for a D or DE. Our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">best dairy-free protein powder</a> covers plant options in depth, though dairy-free and pareve are not the same thing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Egg white</h3>

          <p>
            Eggs are pareve in kosher law, so a certified egg white protein powder is another pareve route with complete, high-quality protein and a neutral taste. Certification is still needed for the finished powder. Our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">egg white protein powder</a> guide covers the products on nutrition.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Collagen and beef protein</h3>

          <p>
            Collagen and beef protein are more complicated. Bovine collagen and beef isolate come from animal hides and tissue, and whether they are kosher depends on the animal and its slaughter; agencies have taken different positions on collagen and gelatin derived from non-kosher sources. Some fish collagen is certified pareve. If a beef protein were certified, it would be meat rather than pareve, which defeats the purpose after a meat meal. In practice, very few collagen or beef protein powders on Amazon carry mainstream certification, so treat them as uncertified unless the symbol says otherwise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="passover" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Kosher Protein Powder Kosher for Passover?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not automatically. Year-round kosher certification and kosher for Passover certification are separate, and a product needs the Passover marking, usually a P next to the symbol or the words kosher for Passover, to be used during the holiday. Note the confusing overlap: on some labels a P after the symbol means Passover, and it never means pareve. Passover rules exclude chametz, leavened grain, and its derivatives, which can appear in protein powders through flavorings, grain alcohol carriers, maltodextrin or other processing aids.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant protein raises a second Passover question. Many Ashkenazi Jews avoid kitniyot during Passover, a category that includes legumes, rice and corn, and pea, rice and soy protein generally fall under it. Sephardic custom often permits kitniyot. So even a pareve pea protein that is kosher year-round may not suit your Passover practice. Whey and egg white avoid kitniyot but still need a Passover certification to cover their flavors and equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            The certifying agencies publish Passover guides each year listing approved products and policies on medications and supplements. Kosher for Passover protein powder is rare among mainstream Amazon brands, so many people simply pause their usual powder for the week and lean on eggs, fish, meat and dairy from certified sources. If you rely on protein supplementation for medical reasons, ask your rabbi and your doctor rather than going without.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="kosher-quality" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Does Kosher Mean a Protein Powder Is Cleaner or Safer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. Kosher certification answers a religious question: are the ingredients, sources and equipment acceptable under kosher law? It does not test for heavy metals, verify the protein content on the label, screen for banned substances or check for microbial contamination. A kosher whey can be under-dosed on protein, and an uncertified whey can be excellent. The two questions are separate, and you should check both.
          </p>

          <p>
            For quality and safety, look at third-party certifications built for supplements. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport test batches for banned substances and verify label claims; the Clean Label Project and Consumer Reports have published heavy-metal results for specific powders. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide summarizes that data, and our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF certified protein powder</a> list covers the sport certifications. The ideal kosher powder carries both kinds of mark.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kosher is also not the same as halal, although the two overlap in some products and some Muslim consumers accept certain kosher products. Halal certification is issued by separate agencies under different rules, particularly on alcohol-based flavor carriers and animal-derived ingredients. If you need halal, look for a halal mark rather than relying on a kosher symbol.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, kosher is not an allergy or diet claim. An OU-D tells you a product contains dairy or was made on dairy equipment, which is useful information for someone avoiding milk, but a pareve symbol is not a guarantee of being free from milk traces for someone with a severe allergy. If you have a food allergy, read the allergen statement and ask the brand, as our <a href="/best-soy-free-protein-powder/">soy-free protein powder</a> guide explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying Kosher Protein Powder on Amazon: Finding the Symbol
          </h2>

          <p>
            Amazon does not verify kosher claims. Sellers can type kosher into a title or bullet point, and some do so for products that carry no recognized agency symbol at all. For a kosher buyer, the listing text is a starting point for a search, never the evidence. The evidence is the agency symbol on the physical label, which you can usually see in the listing images if you look for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Find the symbol in the label images</h3>

          <p>
            Scroll through the listing images and zoom in on the front and back of the tub. Kosher symbols are small and often sit near the nutrition panel, the barcode or the bottom of the front label. Check for the letters next to the symbol, D, DE or P, as well as the symbol itself. If the listing images do not show a symbol, look at the brand's website for the current label, and if the symbol is still missing, assume the product is not certified.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check flavor, size and seller</h3>

          <p>
            Certification can be issued per product, per flavor or per manufacturing plant, and Amazon listings group many flavors and sizes under one page. The headline price and photos may belong to a different flavor or size from the one you have selected, so confirm the selection before trusting either. Buy from the brand or from Amazon directly, shown in the Ships from and Sold by lines; third-party resellers are more likely to send old stock with an outdated label, and a product can gain or lose certification between production runs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check the tub, the lot and the date</h3>

          <p>
            When the tub arrives, read the label again before opening it. Note the lot number and best-by date: if a product's certification has changed, the agency or brand can tell you which lots are covered. Agencies such as the OU and Star-K publish searchable databases and alerts about products sold with unauthorized symbols, and checking the brand name there takes a minute.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Subscribe & Save and cost per 25 g</h3>

          <p>
            Subscribe & Save typically saves 5 percent, and up to 15 percent with five or more subscriptions arriving in the same month. Use it once you have confirmed the symbol on a delivered tub, and recheck the label on later deliveries. To compare prices, divide the price by servings, divide by the protein per serving and multiply by 25 to get cost per 25 g of protein; Devotion's 20 g serving and Orgain's two-scoop serving both cost more per gram than they look. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does the math.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with protein powders genuinely sold on Amazon US and kept those for which the brand, or at minimum consistent retail listings, state kosher certification at the time of writing. We ranked brand statements naming an agency above listing claims without one, and we say which is which on every product. Within that, we ranked on protein per serving, ingredient simplicity, usefulness for dairy and pareve needs, and cost per 25 g of protein. We did not run lab tests or taste panels. See <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> for our method.
          </p>

          <p>
            We are not a kosher authority, and nothing on this page is a ruling. Several popular powders appeared on unofficial kosher lists without any brand statement we could find, and we left them out rather than guess; Soylent, for instance, says its ready-to-drink shakes carry the OU while its powders are not certified. If you have a medical reason to use protein powder, such as recovery from surgery, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-bariatric-surgery/">protein powder for bariatric surgery</a> guide covers that side, and your rabbi can advise on any conflict.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the word kosher in an Amazon title</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Amazon does not verify kosher claims, and listing titles can include the word for products with no recognized agency symbol. Find the symbol itself in the label images, check the letters beside it, and confirm the product in the agency's own database if your community follows a particular agency.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the D next to the symbol</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                OU-D, or a D beside another agency symbol, marks dairy ingredients or dairy equipment. Every whey, casein and milk protein powder is dairy. If you wait after meat before dairy, a dairy-marked shake after a meat lunch can be off-limits for hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming plant-based means pareve</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pea and rice protein are plant foods, but flavors, creamers and shared whey equipment can make a finished plant powder dairy or DE. Treat a plant powder as pareve only when its certification says so, not because its ingredients look dairy-free.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using year-round certification for Passover</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kosher for Passover is a separate certification, usually shown with a P or the words on the label. Pea, rice and soy protein are kitniyot, which many Ashkenazi Jews avoid during Passover. Check your agency's annual Passover guide or ask your rabbi.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating kosher as a quality test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Kosher certification does not test for heavy metals, protein accuracy or banned substances. A kosher powder can still be under-dosed or high in lead. Look for NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or published heavy-metal results alongside the kosher symbol.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best kosher protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  NutraBio 100% Whey Protein Isolate is our top pick because the brand itself lists OU certification, which makes it kosher dairy. Devotion Nutrition states OU-D certification in its FAQ and suits baking. For a plant option, Orgain's site lists its Plant-Based Protein as kosher. Confirm the symbol on the exact flavor you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein kosher?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey can be kosher, but only when an agency certifies the finished powder, because whey comes from cheesemaking and kosher law has rules on cheese production. Kosher whey is always dairy, usually marked OU-D or with a D beside another symbol. You cannot tell from the ingredient list whether a whey is kosher.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What does OU-D mean on protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  OU-D means the Orthodox Union certifies the product as kosher and that it contains dairy ingredients or was made on dairy equipment. For protein powder, it almost always means a whey, casein or milk protein base. People who wait between meat and dairy treat OU-D products as dairy for that purpose.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is there a pareve protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Plant proteins such as pea and rice, and egg white, can be certified pareve, meaning neither meat nor dairy. The finished powder still needs certification, because flavors and shared whey equipment can make it dairy or DE. Check the symbol on the tub; a plant ingredient list alone does not prove pareve status.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I drink a whey protein shake after eating meat?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  If you keep kosher and wait between meat and dairy, a whey shake counts as dairy, so you would wait your community's customary time, often six hours. A certified pareve plant or egg white powder avoids the wait. Customs vary, so follow your own practice or ask your rabbi.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is collagen protein kosher?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Some collagen is certified kosher, but it depends on the animal source and slaughter, and agencies differ on collagen and gelatin from non-kosher sources. Some fish collagen is certified pareve. Most bovine collagen powders on Amazon do not carry mainstream certification, so treat them as uncertified unless the label shows a recognized symbol.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is kosher protein powder kosher for Passover?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not unless it carries a separate kosher for Passover certification, usually a P beside the symbol or the words on the label. Flavors can contain chametz derivatives, and pea, rice and soy protein are kitniyot, which many Ashkenazi Jews avoid on Passover. Kosher for Passover protein powder is rare on Amazon.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
